/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 21-25
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:21, name:"Babylon Falls", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 21:9",verse:"And, behold, here cometh a chariot of men, with a couple of horsemen. And he answered and said, Babylon is fallen, is fallen; and all the graven images of her gods he hath broken unto the ground."}, facts:["Did you know the watchman's cry 'Babylon is fallen, is fallen' is echoed in Revelation 18? John borrowed Isaiah's words to announce the fall of every proud power that sets itself against God.","Did you know Isaiah felt these visions in his own body? He describes pangs like a woman in labor, a panting heart, and sleepless nights, showing that delivering hard truth cost the prophet real suffering.","Did you know Kedar was a tribe of Arabian nomads famed for archery, and Isaiah set a one year deadline on their glory? Their reduction within that single year proved the prophecy's precision."], questions:[
{q:"What does Isaiah call the opening prophecy of chapter 21?", options:["The burden of the desert of the sea","The burden of Dumah","The burden upon Arabia","The burden of the valley of vision"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:1",verse:"The burden of the desert of the sea. As whirlwinds in the south pass through; so it cometh from the desert, from a terrible land.", insight:"The sea desert is Babylon, a dry land far from any ocean. Isaiah names the target cryptically, but God's people knew exactly who was meant."},
{q:"Which two nations does the Lord summon to march against Babylon?", options:["Elam and Media","Assyria and Egypt","Moab and Ammon","Tarshish and Chittim"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:2",verse:"A grievous vision is declared unto me; the treacherous dealer dealeth treacherously, and the spoiler spoileth. Go up, O Elam: besiege, O Media; all the sighing thereof have I made to cease.", insight:"Elam and Media were the peoples who would become Persia. God named Babylon's conquerors more than a century before they rose."},
{q:"What sorrowful phrase does the vision repeat about the traitor?", options:["The treacherous dealer dealeth treacherously","The spoiler spoileth the spoiled","The liar speaketh lies continually","The thief stealeth in the night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:2",verse:"A grievous vision is declared unto me; the treacherous dealer dealeth treacherously, and the spoiler spoileth. Go up, O Elam: besiege, O Media; all the sighing thereof have I made to cease.", insight:"The doubled words paint betrayal as a way of life. When treachery becomes habit, judgment is never far behind."},
{q:"To what does Isaiah compare the pain that seizes him in the vision?", options:["The pangs of a woman that travaileth","The grief of a mourner at a grave","The burning of a fever in the bones","The wound of a sword in battle"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:3",verse:"Therefore are my loins filled with pain: pangs have taken hold upon me, as the pangs of a woman that travaileth: I was bowed down at the hearing of it; I was dismayed at the seeing of it.", insight:"A prophet is not a detached reporter. Isaiah's body ached with the coming sorrow, because love for his people made the warning personal."},
{q:"What happened to the night of Isaiah's pleasure?", options:["It was turned into fear","It was filled with music and dancing","It was cut short by a storm","It was forgotten by morning"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:4",verse:"My heart panted, fearfulness affrighted me: the night of my pleasure hath he turned into fear unto me.", insight:"Even the prophet's rest was stolen by the vision. Comfort cannot survive when God reveals the weight of coming judgment."},
{q:"What are the careless princes told to do as the enemy approaches?", options:["Anoint the shield","Polish the crown","Bar the city gates","Sound the trumpet"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:5",verse:"Prepare the table, watch in the watchtower, eat, drink: arise, ye princes, and anoint the shield.", insight:"Shields were oiled to turn aside blows, a last minute preparation. Partying princes are ordered to ready for war they refused to see coming."},
{q:"What does the Lord command Isaiah to do in verse 6?", options:["Set a watchman to declare what he sees","Build a wall around Jerusalem","Send messengers to Egypt","Number the armies of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:6",verse:"For thus hath the Lord said unto me, Go, set a watchman, let him declare what he seeth.", insight:"God places watchmen on the walls of every generation. Their job is simple and costly: report exactly what they see, whether people listen or not."},
{q:"Besides horsemen, what kinds of chariots does the watchman see approaching?", options:["Chariots of asses and chariots of camels","Chariots of iron and chariots of fire","Chariots of gold and chariots of silver","Chariots of cedar and chariots of oak"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:7",verse:"And he saw a chariot with a couple of horsemen, a chariot of asses, and a chariot of camels; and he hearkened diligently with much heed:", insight:"Asses and camels marked a desert army on the move. The details made the vision verifiable, so hearers could recognize it when it arrived."},
{q:"With what cry does the watchman announce the coming army?", options:["A lion","An eagle","A wolf","A bear"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:8",verse:"And he cried, A lion: My lord, I stand continually upon the watchtower in the daytime, and I am set in my ward whole nights:", insight:"The lion's roar warned of a predator already near. Some watchmen cry alarm too late; this one roared while there was still time to hear."},
{q:"How faithfully does the watchman keep his post?", options:["Continually by day and whole nights on watch","Only when the moon is full","Until the third hour of the night","From Sabbath to Sabbath"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:8",verse:"And he cried, A lion: My lord, I stand continually upon the watchtower in the daytime, and I am set in my ward whole nights:", insight:"Day and night without relief: that is the watchman's calling. Faithful witness is not a shift but a life."},
{q:"What is the watchman's great announcement in verse 9?", options:["Babylon is fallen, is fallen","Jerusalem is saved, is saved","Tyre is broken, is broken","Egypt is humbled, is humbled"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:9",verse:"And, behold, here cometh a chariot of men, with a couple of horsemen. And he answered and said, Babylon is fallen, is fallen; and all the graven images of her gods he hath broken unto the ground.", insight:"The double cry leaves no doubt. The proudest empire of the age fell exactly as God said, and its idols with it."},
{q:"What happened to the graven images of Babylon's gods?", options:["They were broken unto the ground","They were carried into the temple","They were melted into coins","They were hidden in the desert"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:9",verse:"And, behold, here cometh a chariot of men, with a couple of horsemen. And he answered and said, Babylon is fallen, is fallen; and all the graven images of her gods he hath broken unto the ground.", insight:"Idols shatter with the nations that trust them. Whatever we worship besides God will one day lie in pieces at our feet."},
{q:"What burden begins in verse 11?", options:["The burden of Dumah","The burden of Seir","The burden of Edom","The burden of Tema"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:11",verse:"The burden of Dumah. He calleth to me out of Seir, Watchman, what of the night? Watchman, what of the night?", insight:"Dumah means silence, a wordplay on Edom's doom. A nation that chose quiet indifference to God would meet a silence of its own."},
{q:"What question is cried to the watchman out of Seir?", options:["Watchman, what of the night?","Watchman, where is the dawn?","Watchman, who comes to the gate?","Watchman, how long the war?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:11",verse:"The burden of Dumah. He calleth to me out of Seir, Watchman, what of the night? Watchman, what of the night?", insight:"Asked twice, the question trembles with fear. When night is darkest, even proud nations start asking the watchmen for news of morning."},
{q:"How does the watchman answer the question about the night?", options:["The morning cometh, and also the night","The night is past and gone forever","Sleep now, for dawn is certain","Ask again when the stars fade"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:12",verse:"The watchman said, The morning cometh, and also the night: if ye will enquire, enquire ye: return, come.", insight:"Morning comes, but so does another night. God's answer is honest: relief and further trial both lie ahead, so keep enquiring and keep returning."},
{q:"Where do the travelling companies of Dedanim lodge in the burden upon Arabia?", options:["In the forest in Arabia","In the tents of Kedar","In the gates of Tema","In the valleys of Seir"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:13",verse:"The burden upon Arabia. In the forest in Arabia shall ye lodge, O ye travelling companies of Dedanim.", insight:"Merchant caravans forced to camp in the wild woods picture trade routes shattered by war. Prosperity is fragile when God shakes a land."},
{q:"Who brought water and bread to the thirsty fugitives?", options:["The inhabitants of the land of Tema","The princes of the city of Dumah","The merchants of the ships of Tarshish","The elders of the tents of Kedar"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:14",verse:"The inhabitants of the land of Tema brought water to him that was thirsty, they prevented with their bread him that fled.", insight:"Tema showed mercy to the fleeing. In the middle of a judgment oracle, God notes kindness to refugees, and He never forgets it."},
{q:"From what were the fugitives fleeing?", options:["The swords, the drawn sword, and the bent bow","The famine, the pestilence, and the plague","The flood, the fire, and the earthquake","The lion, the bear, and the wolf"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:15",verse:"For they fled from the swords, from the drawn sword, and from the bent bow, and from the grievousness of war.", insight:"War drives people from their homes in every age. Isaiah names the weapons plainly so we feel the terror of those who ran."},
{q:"Within what time frame will all the glory of Kedar fail?", options:["Within a year, according to the years of an hireling","Within a month, according to the moon's course","Within a week, according to the days of labor","Within a day, according to the hours of watch"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:16",verse:"For thus hath the Lord said unto me, Within a year, according to the years of an hireling, and all the glory of Kedar shall fail:", insight:"A hireling's year is exact, not a day more. God's judgments run on His own precise calendar, and Kedar's deadline was set to the day."},
{q:"Which warriors of Kedar will be diminished?", options:["The archers, the mighty men of the children of Kedar","The horsemen, the captains of the chariots","The spearmen, the guards of the gates","The slingers, the keepers of the walls"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 21:17",verse:"And the residue of the number of archers, the mighty men of the children of Kedar, shall be diminished: for the LORD God of Israel hath spoken it.", insight:"Kedar's pride was its bowmen, and God struck their strength first. Whatever a nation trusts most is the first thing judgment touches."}
]},
{n:22, name:"The Valley of Vision", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 22:22",verse:"And the key of the house of David will I lay upon his shoulder; so he shall open, and none shall shut; and he shall shut, and none shall open."}, facts:["Did you know the key of the house of David laid on Eliakim's shoulder (Isaiah 22:22) is quoted by Jesus in Revelation 3:7? The promise that He opens and no one shuts points straight to Christ.","Did you know Hezekiah's engineers really did dig a tunnel to carry the lower pool's water inside Jerusalem's walls? The Siloam Tunnel and its ancient inscription were discovered in 1880, matching Isaiah 22:9-11.","Did you know Jerusalem's party slogan on the eve of judgment was 'let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die'? Paul quotes it in 1 Corinthians 15 to warn against living with no hope beyond the grave."], questions:[
{q:"What does Isaiah call Jerusalem at the start of chapter 22?", options:["The valley of vision","The city of the great King","The mountain of the Lord","The daughter of Zion"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:1",verse:"The burden of the valley of vision. What aileth thee now, that thou art wholly gone up to the housetops?", insight:"Jerusalem saw more visions than any city, yet she would not see her own danger. Privilege without obedience becomes blindness."},
{q:"Where had the people of Jerusalem gone in their panic?", options:["Up to the housetops","Down into the cisterns","Out into the wilderness","Into the house of the forest"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:1",verse:"The burden of the valley of vision. What aileth thee now, that thou art wholly gone up to the housetops?", insight:"Housetops were flat gathering places where people watched the horizon. A whole city climbing to its roofs shows fear has become public."},
{q:"How did the slain of this tumultuous city die?", options:["Not slain with the sword, nor dead in battle","Pierced by arrows at the city gate","Trampled by chariots in the valley","Burned by fire in their houses"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:2",verse:"Thou that art full of stirs, a tumultuous city, a joyous city: thy slain men are not slain with the sword, nor dead in battle.", insight:"They died without fighting, victims of famine or plague during siege. It is a strange shame to fall in war without ever lifting a sword."},
{q:"What happened to all the rulers of Jerusalem?", options:["They fled together and were bound by the archers","They stood firm upon the city walls","They hid in the caves of the mountains","They sailed away in ships of Tarshish"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:3",verse:"All thy rulers are fled together, they are bound by the archers: all that are found in thee are bound together, which have fled from far.", insight:"Leaders who flee first leave their people leaderless. When rulers run, the city they were sworn to protect falls with them."},
{q:"How did Isaiah respond to the ruin of his people?", options:["I will weep bitterly","I will sing a song of triumph","I will gather the elders to feast","I will write it in the chronicles"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:4",verse:"Therefore said I, Look away from me; I will weep bitterly, labour not to comfort me, because of the spoiling of the daughter of my people.", insight:"Isaiah refused cheap comfort and chose bitter tears. Real love for God's people weeps over their ruin instead of explaining it away."},
{q:"What three words describe the day in the valley of vision?", options:["Trouble, treading down, and perplexity","Joy, gladness, and singing","Peace, plenty, and rest","Wisdom, counsel, and might"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:5",verse:"For it is a day of trouble, and of treading down, and of perplexity by the Lord GOD of hosts in the valley of vision, breaking down the walls, and of crying to the mountains.", insight:"Perplexity means no way out that human minds can find. Some days are sent to show us that our own wisdom has run dry."},
{q:"Which two peoples brought the quiver and uncovered the shield against Judah?", options:["Elam and Kir","Assyria and Babylon","Egypt and Cush","Moab and Ammon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:6",verse:"And Elam bare the quiver with chariots of men and horsemen, and Kir uncovered the shield.", insight:"God can raise any nation as His instrument. Elam and Kir were names Judah feared, and the Lord turned their armies toward Jerusalem's gate."},
{q:"What filled the choicest valleys around Jerusalem?", options:["Chariots","Tents of the refugees","Flocks of sheep","Fields of wheat"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:7",verse:"And it shall come to pass, that thy choicest valleys shall be full of chariots, and the horsemen shall set themselves in array at the gate.", insight:"The beautiful valleys became parking grounds for war machines. Judgment has a way of occupying the very places we were proudest of."},
{q:"To what did the people look for protection in that day?", options:["The armour of the house of the forest","The walls of the city of David","The covenant of the Lord of hosts","The prayers of the priests in the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:8",verse:"And he discovered the covering of Judah, and thou didst look in that day to the armour of the house of the forest.", insight:"The house of the forest was Solomon's armory, full of fine weapons. They trusted their arsenal while ignoring the God who gave it."},
{q:"What did the people gather together when they saw the city's breaches?", options:["The waters of the lower pool","The stones of the broken walls","The gold of the merchants' houses","The weapons of the fallen soldiers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:9",verse:"Ye have seen also the breaches of the city of David, that they are many: and ye gathered together the waters of the lower pool.", insight:"They prepared wisely for siege, securing water inside the walls. Human preparation is good, but it becomes folly when it replaces trust in God."},
{q:"Why did the people break down houses in Jerusalem?", options:["To fortify the wall","To build a new palace","To make room for the market","To clear a path for the chariots"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:10",verse:"And ye have numbered the houses of Jerusalem, and the houses have ye broken down to fortify the wall.", insight:"They counted and sacrificed their own homes to strengthen defenses. Desperate times reveal what we will tear down to feel safe."},
{q:"What did the builders of the water ditch fail to do?", options:["Look unto the maker thereof","Pay the laborers their wages","Finish the work before winter","Bless the water in His name"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:11",verse:"Ye made also a ditch between the two walls for the water of the old pool: but ye have not looked unto the maker thereof, neither had respect unto him that fashioned it long ago.", insight:"They engineered a brilliant water system and never glanced at the God who made the water. Skill without reverence is Jerusalem's besetting sin."},
{q:"To what four things did the Lord call His people in that day?", options:["Weeping, mourning, baldness, and sackcloth","Feasting, singing, dancing, and wine","Prayer, fasting, alms, and pilgrimage","Silence, solitude, stillness, and rest"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:12",verse:"And in that day did the Lord GOD of hosts call to weeping, and to mourning, and to baldness, and to girding with sackcloth:", insight:"Baldness and sackcloth were the dress of deep repentance. God asked for broken hearts, and His people answered with a party."},
{q:"What was the people's defiant slogan instead of repentance?", options:["Let us eat and drink; for tomorrow we shall die","Let us build and plant; for the Lord will provide","Let us flee and hide; for the night is coming","Let us watch and pray; for the dawn is near"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:13",verse:"And behold joy and gladness, slaying oxen, and killing sheep, eating flesh, and drinking wine: let us eat and drink; for to morrow we shall die.", insight:"Hopelessness dressed up as joy is the saddest sound in Scripture. When people stop believing in tomorrow with God, they gorge on today."},
{q:"What did the Lord reveal about this iniquity?", options:["It shall not be purged from you till ye die","It shall be forgiven at the next new moon","It shall be forgotten when the siege lifts","It shall be covered by your sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:14",verse:"And it was revealed in mine ears by the LORD of hosts, Surely this iniquity shall not be purged from you till ye die, saith the Lord GOD of hosts.", insight:"Some sins harden past the point of remedy in this life. It is a fearful thing when God says a people's guilt will outlast their generation."},
{q:"What office did Shebna hold in Jerusalem?", options:["Treasurer over the house","Captain of the guard","Keeper of the temple doors","Scribe of the king's chronicles"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:15",verse:"Thus saith the Lord GOD of hosts, Go, get thee unto this treasurer, even unto Shebna, which is over the house, and say,", insight:"Shebna managed the palace, a post of great trust. High office never shields a proud heart from God's searching questions."},
{q:"What was Shebna building for himself in the rock?", options:["A sepulchre hewed out on high","A treasure house for his gold","A watchtower for the city guard","A cistern for the summer drought"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:16",verse:"What hast thou here? and whom hast thou here, that thou hast hewed thee out a sepulchre here, as he that heweth him out a sepulchre on high, and that graveth an habitation for himself in a rock?", insight:"He carved a lofty tomb to be remembered forever. Men who build monuments to themselves often lose the very legacy they craved."},
{q:"How would the Lord toss Shebna in judgment?", options:["Like a ball into a large country","Like chaff before the wind","Like a stone into the deep sea","Like dust upon the threshing floor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:18",verse:"He will surely violently turn and toss thee like a ball into a large country: there shalt thou die, and there the chariots of thy glory shall be the shame of thy lord’s house.", insight:"The proud treasurer who built high would be hurled far. God's picture of judgment is vivid on purpose: pride always ends in a hard fall."},
{q:"Whom would God call to replace Shebna?", options:["Eliakim the son of Hilkiah","Joab the son of Zeruiah","Azariah the son of Nathan","Jehoshaphat the recorder"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:20",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that I will call my servant Eliakim the son of Hilkiah:", insight:"God named the replacement before the removal. He always has His servant ready, and no proud official is indispensable to His plans."},
{q:"What would Eliakim be to the people of Jerusalem?", options:["A father to the inhabitants of Jerusalem","A warrior before the armies of Judah","A judge in the gates of the city","A prophet to the house of David"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 22:21",verse:"And I will clothe him with thy robe, and strengthen him with thy girdle, and I will commit thy government into his hand: and he shall be a father to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and to the house of Judah.", insight:"A father, not a tyrant: that is God's model for leadership. Authority given by God is meant to shelter, not to strut."}
]}
,
{n:23, name:"The Fall of Tyre", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 23:9",verse:"The LORD of hosts hath purposed it, to stain the pride of all glory, and to bring into contempt all the honourable of the earth."}, facts:["Did you know Tyre's merchants were so rich that Isaiah calls them princes and the honourable of the earth? Yet God purposed to stain all that glory in a single stroke, proving wealth is no shelter.","Did you know Tyre was forgotten for seventy years, the same span as Judah's exile in Babylon? Scripture often ties seventy years to God's measured seasons of judgment and restoration.","Did you know this judgment ends with grace? Tyre's merchandise becomes holiness to the Lord, feeding His people, a promise that even the proudest trading city can be redeemed."], questions:[
{q:"What does Isaiah command the ships of Tarshish to do?", options:["Howl, for Tyre is laid waste","Set sail, for the winds are fair","Return home, for the seas are calm","Give thanks, for the voyage prospers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:1",verse:"The burden of Tyre. Howl, ye ships of Tarshish; for it is laid waste, so that there is no house, no entering in: from the land of Chittim it is revealed to them.", insight:"Howling was the cry of those whose livelihood sank with the city. When God judges a trading empire, its ships feel it first."},
{q:"From what land is the news of Tyre's ruin revealed?", options:["From the land of Chittim","From the land of Egypt","From the land of Tarshish","From the land of Arabia"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:1",verse:"The burden of Tyre. Howl, ye ships of Tarshish; for it is laid waste, so that there is no house, no entering in: from the land of Chittim it is revealed to them.", insight:"Chittim was Cyprus, the first port west of Tyre. Bad news travels by ship, and sailors carried Tyre's obituary across the sea."},
{q:"Who had replenished the inhabitants of the isle?", options:["The merchants of Zidon that pass over the sea","The sailors of the ships of Tarshish","The princes of the land of Chittim","The traffickers of the cities of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:2",verse:"Be still, ye inhabitants of the isle; thou whom the merchants of Zidon, that pass over the sea, have replenished.", insight:"Tyre's wealth floated in on other men's ships. A city built on passing trade discovers how quickly the passing can stop."},
{q:"What was Tyre's revenue, according to verse 3?", options:["The seed of Sihor, the harvest of the river","The silver of Tarshish, the gold of Ophir","The cedars of Lebanon, the oaks of Bashan","The flocks of Kedar, the herds of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:3",verse:"And by great waters the seed of Sihor, the harvest of the river, is her revenue; and she is a mart of nations.", insight:"Sihor is the Nile, and Egypt's grain filled Tyre's storehouses. Her riches were imported, which made her prosperity borrowed and brittle."},
{q:"Why is Zidon told to be ashamed?", options:["For the sea hath spoken against her","For her walls have fallen flat","For her king has fled by night","For her fields lie untilled"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:4",verse:"Be thou ashamed, O Zidon: for the sea hath spoken, even the strength of the sea, saying, I travail not, nor bring forth children, neither do I nourish up young men, nor bring up virgins.", insight:"Even the sea, Tyre's old ally, testifies against her. When creation itself joins the prosecution, there is no defense left."},
{q:"How will the nations react at the report of Tyre?", options:["They shall be sorely pained, as at the report of Egypt","They shall rejoice and sing for joy","They shall send ambassadors with gifts","They shall forget it by the next new moon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:5",verse:"As at the report concerning Egypt, so shall they be sorely pained at the report of Tyre.", insight:"Egypt's plagues had taught the world to tremble at God's judgments. Tyre's fall would ring the same alarm in every trading port."},
{q:"Where are the inhabitants of the isle told to flee?", options:["Pass ye over to Tarshish","Flee ye into the wilderness","Hide ye in the caves of the rocks","Sail ye to the isles of Chittim"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:6",verse:"Pass ye over to Tarshish; howl, ye inhabitants of the isle.", insight:"Tarshish was the far west, the end of the known world. When home collapses, people run to the farthest place they can name."},
{q:"How does verse 7 describe the once joyous city?", options:["Her own feet shall carry her afar off to sojourn","Her own hands shall rebuild her fallen walls","Her own ships shall return laden with gold","Her own sons shall sing in her streets"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:7",verse:"Is this your joyous city, whose antiquity is of ancient days? her own feet shall carry her afar off to sojourn.", insight:"The city that sent ships everywhere would walk into exile on foot. Pride's favorite punishment is to travel the road it once ruled."},
{q:"What title does Isaiah give Tyre in verse 8?", options:["The crowning city","The joyous city","The merchant city","The ancient city"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:8",verse:"Who hath taken this counsel against Tyre, the crowning city, whose merchants are princes, whose traffickers are the honourable of the earth?", insight:"Tyre crowned other cities with colonies and trade. The crowning city would learn that only God gives crowns, and only He takes them away."},
{q:"Whose merchants are called princes and the honourable of the earth?", options:["Tyre's","Zidon's","Tarshish's","Egypt's"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:8",verse:"Who hath taken this counsel against Tyre, the crowning city, whose merchants are princes, whose traffickers are the honourable of the earth?", insight:"Trade had made merchants into royalty. Wealth can buy the honors of earth, but it cannot buy honor in the day of judgment."},
{q:"Why did the Lord purpose Tyre's humiliation?", options:["To stain the pride of all glory","To reward the faith of the poor","To test the patience of the sailors","To enrich the kings of the east"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:9",verse:"The LORD of hosts hath purposed it, to stain the pride of all glory, and to bring into contempt all the honourable of the earth.", insight:"Pride is the one thing God always stains. Every glory that exalts itself will be brought low; that is as certain as sunrise."},
{q:"What will the daughter of Tarshish no longer have?", options:["Strength","Ships","Silver","Sailors"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:10",verse:"Pass through thy land as a river, O daughter of Tarshish: there is no more strength.", insight:"Strength was Tarshish's boast, its fleets and fortunes. God removes the very thing a people trusts instead of Him."},
{q:"What did the Lord command against the merchant city?", options:["To destroy the strong holds thereof","To burn the ships in the harbor","To scatter the gold in the streets","To silence the songs of the sailors"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:11",verse:"He stretched out his hand over the sea, he shook the kingdoms: the LORD hath given a commandment against the merchant city, to destroy the strong holds thereof.", insight:"Strongholds are the last thing a proud city surrenders. God aims His command at the fortress of self confidence itself."},
{q:"Where is the oppressed daughter of Zidon told to go, and what will she find?", options:["Pass over to Chittim, where she shall have no rest","Flee to Egypt, where she shall find plenty","Sail to Tarshish, where she shall find peace","Hide in Arabia, where she shall find shade"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:12",verse:"And he said, Thou shalt no more rejoice, O thou oppressed virgin, daughter of Zidon: arise, pass over to Chittim; there also shalt thou have no rest.", insight:"There is no rest for the judged, not even in refuge. Running from God's sentence only lengthens the road of sorrow."},
{q:"Who founded the land of the Chaldeans for desert dwellers?", options:["The Assyrian","The Egyptian","The Persian","The Hittite"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:13",verse:"Behold the land of the Chaldeans; this people was not, til the Assyrian founded it for them that dwell in the wilderness: they set up the towers thereof, they raised up the palaces thereof; and he brought it to ruin.", insight:"Assyria built up Babylon, then Babylon outgrew its maker. Empires raise the very powers that will one day bury them."},
{q:"For how long will Tyre be forgotten?", options:["Seventy years","Forty years","Seven years","One hundred years"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:15",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that Tyre shall be forgotten seventy years, according to the days of one king: after the end of seventy years shall Tyre sing as an harlot.", insight:"Seventy years is a lifetime, long enough for a generation to forget a city's name. God measures oblivion as carefully as He measures mercy."},
{q:"After seventy years, what will Tyre sing like?", options:["As an harlot","As a bride","As a warrior","As a child"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:15",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that Tyre shall be forgotten seventy years, according to the days of one king: after the end of seventy years shall Tyre sing as an harlot.", insight:"The harlot image is harsh because Tyre sold herself to every nation for profit. God calls commercial seduction by its true name."},
{q:"What is the forgotten harlot told to take up and do?", options:["Take an harp and sing many songs to be remembered","Take a sword and fight for her freedom","Take a crown and reclaim her throne","Take a veil and hide her shame"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:16",verse:"Take an harp, go about the city, thou harlot that hast been forgotten; make sweet melody, sing many songs, that thou mayest be remembered.", insight:"Tyre would hawk her old songs like a street singer begging attention. Fallen glory always tries to sing its way back."},
{q:"What will the Lord do for Tyre after the seventy years?", options:["The Lord will visit Tyre","The Lord will forsake Tyre","The Lord will forget Tyre","The Lord will consume Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:17",verse:"And it shall come to pass after the end of seventy years, that the LORD will visit Tyre, and she shall turn to her hire, and shall commit fornication with all the kingdoms of the world upon the face of the earth.", insight:"Visit here means restore to activity, and even that is grace. God's judgments end, but His mercy keeps the last word."},
{q:"What will Tyre's merchandise become in the end?", options:["Holiness to the Lord","A spoil for the nations","Dust upon the ground","A memorial of her shame"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 23:18",verse:"And her merchandise and her hire shall be holiness to the LORD: it shall not be treasured nor laid up; for her merchandise shall be for them that dwell before the LORD, to eat sufficiently, and for durable clothing.", insight:"The wealth that once fed pride will feed God's people. Redemption does not destroy our gifts; it redirects them to holiness."}
]}
,
{n:24, name:"The Whole Earth Judged", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 24:23",verse:"Then the moon shall be confounded, and the sun ashamed, when the LORD of hosts shall reign in mount Zion, and in Jerusalem, and before his ancients gloriously."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 24 is sometimes called the little apocalypse? It pictures the entire earth judged at once, a preview of the end times scenes in Revelation.","Did you know judgment here falls on everyone alike, priest and people, master and servant, buyer and seller? No rank, wealth, or office escapes God's justice.","Did you know this dark chapter ends in blazing hope? The Lord of hosts will reign gloriously in Jerusalem, and even the sun and moon will be outshone by His glory."], questions:[
{q:"What three things does the Lord do to the earth in verse 1?", options:["Make it empty, make it waste, and turn it upside down","Fill it with people, bless it with rain, and crown it with peace","Shake the mountains, dry the seas, and darken the sun","Scatter the stars, silence the winds, and still the rivers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:1",verse:"Behold, the LORD maketh the earth empty, and maketh it waste, and turneth it upside down, and scattereth abroad the inhabitants thereof.", insight:"Empty, waste, upside down: creation unraveled. The God who ordered the world can disorder it when its people rebel."},
{q:"Which pair shows that judgment falls on all ranks alike?", options:["As with the people, so with the priest","As with the king, so with the prophet","As with the rich, so with the judge","As with the elder, so with the child"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:2",verse:"And it shall be, as with the people, so with the priest; as with the servant, so with his master; as with the maid, so with her mistress; as with the buyer, so with the seller; as with the lender, so with the borrower; as with the taker of usury, so with the giver of usury to him.", insight:"Priest and people stand in the same dock. Religious office is no shelter when God judges the heart."},
{q:"How completely will the land be emptied?", options:["Utterly emptied, and utterly spoiled","Half emptied, and partly spoiled","Emptied of strangers, but spared for friends","Spoiled of gold, but rich in grain"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:3",verse:"The land shall be utterly emptied, and utterly spoiled: for the LORD hath spoken this word.", insight:"Utterly is repeated because partial judgment would not suffice. God's sentence leaves no loophole for the impenitent."},
{q:"Who are the ones that languish as the earth fades?", options:["The haughty people of the earth","The humble poor of the land","The little children of the villages","The aged elders of the cities"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:4",verse:"The earth mourneth and fadeth away, the world languisheth and fadeth away, the haughty people of the earth do languish.", insight:"Haughtiness withers first. Pride looks strong in good times, but it has no roots for the day of drought."},
{q:"Why is the earth defiled under its inhabitants?", options:["They have transgressed the laws, changed the ordinance, broken the everlasting covenant","They have forgotten the feasts, neglected the temple, and silenced the singers","They have built high towers, dug deep wells, and planted vineyards","They have counted their gold, weighed their silver, and stored their grain"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:5",verse:"The earth also is defiled under the inhabitants thereof; because they have transgressed the laws, changed the ordinance, broken the everlasting covenant.", insight:"The earth itself suffers for human sin. Our rebellion does not stay private; it poisons the ground beneath our feet."},
{q:"What has devoured the earth, leaving few men?", options:["The curse","The famine","The locust","The sword"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:6",verse:"Therefore hath the curse devoured the earth, and they that dwell therein are desolate: therefore the inhabitants of the earth are burned, and few men left.", insight:"The curse pronounced in Eden still works through history. Sin's wages are not only personal; they are planetary."},
{q:"What mourns and what languishes in verse 7?", options:["The new wine mourns, the vine languishes","The old olive mourns, the fig tree languishes","The wheat field mourns, the barley languishes","The pomegranate mourns, the palm tree languishes"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:7",verse:"The new wine mourneth, the vine languisheth, all the merryhearted do sigh.", insight:"Even the harvest grieves. When joy is judged, the very crops seem to hang their heads."},
{q:"What three sounds of joy come to an end?", options:["The tabret, the rejoicing noise, and the harp","The trumpet, the shouting, and the psaltery","The pipe, the dancing, and the timbrel","The cymbal, the singing, and the viol"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:8",verse:"The mirth of tabrets ceaseth, the noise of them that rejoice endeth, the joy of the harp ceaseth.", insight:"Music was the heartbeat of celebration, and God silences it. There is a grief so deep that songs simply stop."},
{q:"What happens to strong drink in the day of judgment?", options:["It shall be bitter to them that drink it","It shall be sweet as honey to the thirsty","It shall be poured out upon the ground","It shall be forgotten in the cellars"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:9",verse:"They shall not drink wine with a song; strong drink shall be bitter to them that drink it.", insight:"Even escape through drink turns bitter. When God judges, the anesthetics stop working."},
{q:"What is the city in verse 10 called?", options:["The city of confusion","The city of destruction","The city of desolation","The city of sorrow"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:10",verse:"The city of confusion is broken down: every house is shut up, that no man may come in.", insight:"Confusion is Babel's old name for itself. Every society built without God ends as a city of confusion, locked from the inside."},
{q:"For what is there crying in the streets?", options:["For wine","For bread","For water","For mercy"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:11",verse:"There is a crying for wine in the streets; all joy is darkened, the mirth of the land is gone.", insight:"They cry for wine, not for God. Misery still reaches for the old comforts instead of the only true Comforter."},
{q:"What is left in the city, and what is smitten?", options:["Desolation is left, and the gate is smitten with destruction","Silence is left, and the wall is smitten with breaches","Darkness is left, and the tower is smitten with fire","Emptiness is left, and the market is smitten with famine"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:12",verse:"In the city is left desolation, and the gate is smitten with destruction.", insight:"The gate was the city's pride and protection. When God smites the gate, He touches the very symbol of human security."},
{q:"To what is the remnant among the people compared?", options:["The shaking of an olive tree and gleaning grapes when the vintage is done","The sifting of wheat and winnowing barley at harvest","The pruning of a vine and gathering figs in summer","The shearing of sheep and carding wool in spring"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:13",verse:"When thus it shall be in the midst of the land among the people, there shall be as the shaking of an olive tree, and as the gleaning grapes when the vintage is done.", insight:"Gleanings are the few fruits left after harvest. God's remnant is small but precious, the olives shaken loose yet still His."},
{q:"From where do the joyful lift their voices for the majesty of the Lord?", options:["From the sea","From the mountains","From the wilderness","From the cities"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:14",verse:"They shall lift up their voice, they shall sing for the majesty of the LORD, they shall cry aloud from the sea.", insight:"Even in worldwide judgment, some sing. The remnant's song rises from the sea, proof that worship outlives wrath."},
{q:"In what two places are we told to glorify the Lord?", options:["In the fires, and in the isles of the sea","In the temple, and in the high places","In the morning, and in the evening","In the city, and in the field"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:15",verse:"Wherefore glorify ye the LORD in the fires, even the name of the LORD God of Israel in the isles of the sea.", insight:"In the fires: praise is commanded even in the furnace. The isles of the sea: no place is too far for God's glory to reach."},
{q:"What song is heard from the uttermost part of the earth?", options:["Glory to the righteous","Peace to the humble","Mercy to the contrite","Honor to the mighty"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:16",verse:"From the uttermost part of the earth have we heard songs, even glory to the righteous. But I said, My leanness, my leanness, woe unto me! the treacherous dealers have dealt treacherously; yea, the treacherous dealers have dealt very treacherously.", insight:"The ends of the earth sing of the Righteous One. Hope is never local; it echoes from the farthest shore."},
{q:"What three terrors are upon the inhabitant of the earth?", options:["Fear, and the pit, and the snare","War, and famine, and pestilence","Fire, and hail, and darkness","Sword, and hunger, and exile"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:17",verse:"Fear, and the pit, and the snare, are upon thee, O inhabitant of the earth.", insight:"Fear drives into the pit, and the pit leads to the snare. Fleeing God is a maze with no exit, only deeper traps."},
{q:"What happens to the one who climbs out of the pit?", options:["He shall be taken in the snare","He shall find a hiding place","He shall be lifted by angels","He shall escape into the night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:18",verse:"And it shall come to pass, that he who fleeth from the noise of the fear shall fall into the pit; and he that cometh up out of the midst of the pit shall be taken in the snare: for the windows from on high are open, and the foundations of the earth do shake.", insight:"There is no outwitting this judgment. Every escape route has been mapped, and the only safe place is surrender to God."},
{q:"To what is the reeling earth compared in verse 20?", options:["A drunkard, and a cottage removed","A ship, and a sail torn","A reed, and a staff broken","A leaf, and a flower faded"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:20",verse:"The earth shall reel to and fro like a drunkard, and shall be removed like a cottage; and the transgression thereof shall be heavy upon it; and it shall fall, and not rise again.", insight:"A drunkard cannot walk straight, and a flimsy hut cannot stand. The solid earth will stagger like both when its transgression weighs it down."},
{q:"Whom will the Lord punish in that day, besides the kings of the earth?", options:["The host of the high ones that are on high","The princes of the sea and the isles","The merchants of Tarshish and Zidon","The watchmen of the night and day"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 24:21",verse:"And it shall come to pass in that day, that the LORD shall punish the host of the high ones that are on high, and the kings of the earth upon the earth.", insight:"Judgment reaches beyond human kings to the spiritual powers behind them. No throne, seen or unseen, is above God's court."}
]}
,
{n:25, name:"A Feast for All Peoples", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 25:8",verse:"He will swallow up death in victory; and the Lord GOD will wipe away tears from off all faces; and the rebuke of his people shall he take away from off all the earth: for the LORD hath spoken it."}, facts:["Did you know 'He will swallow up death in victory' (Isaiah 25:8) is quoted by Paul in 1 Corinthians 15? It is the Bible's great shout of resurrection hope, first sung by Isaiah centuries before Christ.","Did you know the feast on this mountain is made for all people, not Israel only? It is one of the clearest promises in the Old Testament that God's salvation is for every nation.","Did you know the tears God wipes from all faces here reappear in Revelation 21? John's vision of the new earth finishes the promise Isaiah started: sorrow will finally end."], questions:[
{q:"What two things does Isaiah vow to do for God's name in verse 1?", options:["Exalt thee and praise thy name","Serve thee and follow thy ways","Bless thee and keep thy law","Seek thee and find thy face"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:1",verse:"O LORD, thou art my God; I will exalt thee, I will praise thy name; for thou hast done wonderful things; thy counsels of old are faithfulness and truth.", insight:"Exalting lifts God high in our words; praising tells what He has done. Worship is both adoration and testimony."},
{q:"Why will Isaiah praise the Lord?", options:["For thou hast done wonderful things","For thou hast given me long life","For thou hast made me a prophet","For thou hast heard my prayer"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:1",verse:"O LORD, thou art my God; I will exalt thee, I will praise thy name; for thou hast done wonderful things; thy counsels of old are faithfulness and truth.", insight:"Wonderful things: God's works are the fuel of praise. We do not praise Him for nothing; we praise Him because He acts."},
{q:"What has the Lord made of the defenced city?", options:["A ruin, never to be built","A garden, watered by springs","A temple, filled with singers","A market, crowded with traders"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:2",verse:"For thou hast made of a city an heap; of a defenced city a ruin: a palace of strangers to be no city; it shall never be built.", insight:"The palace of strangers becomes no city at all. Human fortresses that defy God end as heaps, never to rise again."},
{q:"Who will glorify God and fear Him, according to verse 3?", options:["The strong people and the city of the terrible nations","The meek people and the village of the humble","The wise people and the school of the learned","The rich people and the house of the merchants"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:3",verse:"Therefore shall the strong people glorify thee, the city of the terrible nations shall fear thee.", insight:"Even terrible nations will bow. God's judgments are evangelistic: they turn the strong toward worship."},
{q:"What four shelters is God to the poor and needy?", options:["Strength, strength, refuge from the storm, and shadow from the heat","Bread, water, clothing, and rest","Tower, wall, gate, and shield","Light, song, oil, and wine"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:4",verse:"For thou hast been a strength to the poor, a strength to the needy in his distress, a refuge from the storm, a shadow from the heat, when the blast of the terrible ones is as a storm against the wall.", insight:"Twice called strength, then refuge, then shadow: God meets every exposure of the poor. He is the poor man's whole defense system."},
{q:"To what is the blast of the terrible ones compared?", options:["A storm against the wall","A fire among the stubble","A flood over the plain","A hammer upon the rock"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:4",verse:"For thou hast been a strength to the poor, a strength to the needy in his distress, a refuge from the storm, a shadow from the heat, when the blast of the terrible ones is as a storm against the wall.", insight:"The terrible beat against God's people like wind on a wall, but the wall is God Himself. Storms spend themselves on Him."},
{q:"How will God bring down the noise of strangers?", options:["As the heat in a dry place, with the shadow of a cloud","As the night with the rising of the dawn","As the winter with the coming of the spring","As the sea with the calming of the wind"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:5",verse:"Thou shalt bring down the noise of strangers, as the heat in a dry place; even the heat with the shadow of a cloud: the branch of the terrible ones shall be brought low.", insight:"A cloud's shadow cools scorching heat in an instant. God's relief is that swift and that complete when He moves."},
{q:"Where will the Lord of hosts make a feast for all people?", options:["In this mountain","In the valley of vision","In the city of David","In the land of Goshen"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:6",verse:"And in this mountain shall the LORD of hosts make unto all people a feast of fat things, a feast of wines on the lees, of fat things full of marrow, of wines on the lees well refined.", insight:"This mountain is Zion, where God dwells with His people. Salvation has an address, and the invitation goes out from there."},
{q:"What two rich foods are named at the feast?", options:["Fat things full of marrow and wines on the lees well refined","Bread of wheat and honey from the rock","Figs of the valley and pomegranates of the garden","Milk of the flock and oil of the olive"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:6",verse:"And in this mountain shall the LORD of hosts make unto all people a feast of fat things, a feast of wines on the lees, of fat things full of marrow, of wines on the lees well refined.", insight:"Marrow was the richest cut, aged wine the finest drink. God does not serve leftovers at His table; He serves the best."},
{q:"What will the Lord destroy in this mountain?", options:["The face of the covering cast over all people","The walls of the cities of the nations","The idols of the gods of the earth","The chains of the prisoners in darkness"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:7",verse:"And he will destroy in this mountain the face of the covering cast over all people, and the vail that is spread over all nations.", insight:"The covering is spiritual blindness over every nation. God's salvation begins by tearing the veil so people can finally see."},
{q:"What will God swallow up in victory?", options:["Death","Sorrow","Fear","Night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:8",verse:"He will swallow up death in victory; and the Lord GOD will wipe away tears from off all faces; and the rebuke of his people shall he take away from off all the earth: for the LORD hath spoken it.", insight:"Death itself will be devoured by a greater power. The last enemy becomes the final meal of God's victory."},
{q:"What will the Lord God wipe away from off all faces?", options:["Tears","Shame","Dust","Sweat"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:8",verse:"He will swallow up death in victory; and the Lord GOD will wipe away tears from off all faces; and the rebuke of his people shall he take away from off all the earth: for the LORD hath spoken it.", insight:"God's own hand dries every eye. The tenderness of this picture is staggering: the Judge of all becomes the comforter of each."},
{q:"What will God's people say in that day?", options:["Lo, this is our God; we have waited for him","Behold, this is our king; we have crowned him","Hear, this is our law; we have kept it","See, this is our temple; we have built it"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:9",verse:"And it shall be said in that day, Lo, this is our God; we have waited for him, and he will save us: this is the LORD; we have waited for him, we will be glad and rejoice in his salvation.", insight:"Waiting is the quiet work of faith, and this is its payday. Those who waited longest will shout loudest."},
{q:"What will the waiting ones do in His salvation?", options:["Be glad and rejoice","Weep and mourn","Kneel and tremble","Watch and fast"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:9",verse:"And it shall be said in that day, Lo, this is our God; we have waited for him, and he will save us: this is the LORD; we have waited for him, we will be glad and rejoice in his salvation.", insight:"Gladness and rejoicing are the proper clothes of the saved. Heaven's mood is joy, and the waiting saints finally wear it."},
{q:"Where shall the hand of the Lord rest?", options:["In this mountain","Upon the waters of the sea","Over the fields of the valley","Beside the rivers of Eden"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:10",verse:"For in this mountain shall the hand of the LORD rest, and Moab shall be trodden down under him, even as straw is trodden down for the dunghill.", insight:"God's hand rests where His feast is spread. His presence settles permanently on Zion, never to lift again."},
{q:"To what is Moab's treading down compared?", options:["Straw trodden down for the dunghill","Grapes trodden in the winepress","Clay trodden by the potter","Dust trodden by the wind"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:10",verse:"For in this mountain shall the hand of the LORD rest, and Moab shall be trodden down under him, even as straw is trodden down for the dunghill.", insight:"The image is deliberately humiliating: Moab, proud neighbor, ends as straw in manure. Contempt for God earns contempt's own reward."},
{q:"How does Moab spread forth his hands in the midst of the waters?", options:["As he that swimmeth spreadeth forth his hands to swim","As he that reapeth gathereth the sheaves to bind","As he that buildeth lifteth the stones to lay","As he that prayeth raiseth his arms to heaven"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:11",verse:"And he shall spread forth his hands in the midst of them, as he that swimmeth spreadeth forth his hands to swim: and he shall bring down their pride together with the spoils of their hands.", insight:"Moab flails like a drowning swimmer, proud even while sinking. Pride keeps stroking when it should be crying for rescue."},
{q:"What will God bring down together with the spoils of their hands?", options:["Their pride","Their riches","Their armies","Their idols"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:11",verse:"And he shall spread forth his hands in the midst of them, as he that swimmeth spreadeth forth his hands to swim: and he shall bring down their pride together with the spoils of their hands.", insight:"God targets pride along with plunder. He is not after their goods so much as the arrogance that clutched them."},
{q:"What will happen to the fortress of the high fort of thy walls?", options:["He shall bring it down to the dust","He shall set it on fire with brimstone","He shall give it to the beasts of the field","He shall wash it away with the flood"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:12",verse:"And the fortress of the high fort of thy walls shall he bring down, lay low, and bring to the ground, even to the dust.", insight:"Down, lay low, to the ground, to the dust: four verbs for total collapse. No fortress stands when God decides it falls."},
{q:"Over whom is the covering cast that the Lord will destroy?", options:["Over all people, and the vail over all nations","Over the kings of the earth only","Over the city of Jerusalem alone","Over the armies of Moab in battle"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 25:7",verse:"And he will destroy in this mountain the face of the covering cast over all people, and the vail that is spread over all nations.", insight:"All people, all nations: the veil of spiritual blindness is universal, and so is the grace that lifts it."}
]}
]);
